"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Favorable findings include negative surgical margins, absence of seminal vesicle invasion, absence of lymphovascular invasion, and no lymph node metastasis. Conversely, the preoperative PSA was elevated at 5, and the biopsy demonstrated a higher grade (Gleason 4+4) than the prostatectomy specimen (Gleason 3+4). The absence of nodal metastasis is a positive prognostic factor. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data significantly widens the uncertainty regarding future trajectory. The documented risk horizon suggests a potential recurrence within a range of 27 to 41 months. Information regarding the postoperative PSA level and its subsequent trajectory is crucial for refining this assessment. The fixed estimated horizon is 33.86 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 27.09–40.63 months; its coverage has not been validated."
